1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Christiane Lange I, born at Akershus on 1 March 1795, baptized in the castle 
2 CONT
2 CONT chapel on 31 March 1795. Godparents: Madame Moestue, Madame Bagge, maiden 
2 CONT
2 CONT Wold, Officer Justice Department Berg, Royal Cabinet Wexels and Director Ingstad. 
2 CONT
2 CONT After her husbands death, she lived for many year in the old townhouse "Oestre Gade No. 
2 CONT
2 CONT 9", but later moved to her son-in-law, professor Gisle Johnson at his estate "Breidablik" 
2 CONT
2 CONT by the old Aker church. She died there on 3 November 1885 (90 1/2 year old) and was 
2 CONT
2 CONT buried on 9 a.m. at the family site at the "Vor Frelsers Gravlund" cemetery in Christiania. 
2 CONT
2 CONT During her last years as she had during her younger years, she suffered from insanity with 
2 CONT
2 CONT some good days in between. Her daughter, Mrs. Emilie Johnson, has published "Nogle 
2 CONT
2 CONT Optegnelser Fra Christiane Dybwads Syge- og Doedsleie", printed as manuscript in 1907.
2 CONT
2 CONT Married in the Gran Church on 23 September 1814 to at that time, sign Writer Jacob 
2 CONT
2 CONT Erasmus Dybwad, born at Aas in Hakedal on 20 October 1792, died in Christiania on 31 
2 CONT
2 CONT July 1854. He was the son of clerk (working for Bernt Anker) Bertram Dybwad (born on 
2 CONT
2 CONT 24 April 1768, died on 8 November 1844) and Abel Margrethe LefŠvre (born on 10 
2 CONT
2 CONT August 1767, died on 26 September 1849). Abel Margrethe LefŠvre was the daughter of a 
2 CONT
2 CONT French immigrant who had settled as a wig maker in Christiania - Jacob E. Dybwas 
2 CONT
2 CONT worked as clerk for Count H. Wedel-Jartsberg and lived at V‘kkero until he got 
2 CONT
2 CONT citizenship as a merchant in Christiania on 15 October 1824. He opened his business on 
2 CONT
2 CONT Easter Eve, 2 April 1825, in what was at that time called "Ostre Gate No. 9", later called 
2 CONT
2 CONT "Dybwads Garden", Carl Johans Gate 3. The name of the firm was later changed to "J. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dybwad & Sons" and the business started, as many others, as a shop for farmers, but later
2 CONT
2 CONT developed into a banking business. 13 children. (No. 78-90) (Ref. "Dybwads Family-
2 CONT
2 CONT Almanac". Christiania 1885 and Bertram Dybwad: Tamilien Dybwad med 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sidegrenene11, Christiania 1911 and Tillaeg til samme', 1912.)
