1 NAME Berent von Krogh, Stadsmajor
1 NAME Bernhardus //
2 GIVN Bernhardus
2 SURN von Krogh
2 NICK Berent von Krogh
1 NAME Berent von Krogh, Stadsmajor
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1620
2 PLAC Lübeck, Schleswig Holstein, Germany
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 9 AUG 1676
2 ADDR
3 CITY Trondheim
3 STAE Sør-Trøndelag, Trøndelag
3 CTRY Norge
1 OCCU Kom til Norge på oppdrag fra den dansk-norske konge, sjef for leiesoldater fra Bremen, ble stadsmajor i Trondheim i 1644
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} I flge Trondheims historie, bind 2 fikk Bent v. Krogh konglig privelegium til å ha vertshus utenfor  byens  grenser, dette var en vanlig kilde til inntekt for offiserer i de dager. I tillegg hadde han monopol i Trø
2 CONC ndelag for import av  div. viner, bl a Rhinskvin.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT This family is thought to have it;s origin from the area of Osterstade near Bremen. Germany. It;s name spelled variously as Krogh, Kroge, Kroghe, Krogen, Krough, Krug, Croghe, Croge, etc., is very old among German nobility indeed.
2 CONC  It appears as early as 1236. A genealogical Table from the 1700's takes the family back to the year 1400. Ths table, however, is not available or verifiable. However, the family has, on specific occasions been recognized as nobil
2 CONC ity by the President of the Duchy of Bremen Knighthood in a document, dated June 8, 1870, and by the Royal Heraldic District in Berlin in a document, dated June 4, 1875. Similarly the family has been recognized as noble in Denmark
2 CONC  and have occupied high military and civilian positions. They also have intermarried with the most highly esteemed noble families.
2 CONT
2 CONT The family Coat of Arms has been a white castle with two towers between which are four rising golden morning stars against a blue background, and on the crest a greyhound, half white, with a golden collar in between two blue and w
2 CONC hite wings. 
2 CONT
2 CONT The family's ancestor in Denmark and Norway wrote his name as BERENT von KROGH, but his contemporaries called him Berent von Kroge or von Krueg. He was the son of GEORG (JURGEN) von Krogh and a Captain of a company located in Gluc
2 CONC kstadt in 1645. At King Frederick III's succession to the throne, he appears to have entered Danish-Norwegian service on a permanent basis. It is possible, however, that he is not the first of his family to come to Denmark. In 165
2 CONC 3 Gehard(Gert) von Krogh, an older brother of Bernhardus, died in Trondheim. He and his wife, Alked, had three sons and three daughters, but his descendants can only be traced to the third generation. At the same time Heinrich von
2 CONC  Krogh, died Oct. 8, 1655, a younger brother of Bernhardus living in Lybaek. He was married to Anna Hasse. One of his sons , named Heinrich, born in 1655 in Lybaek, was a medical graduate. He died Dec. 6, 1679 in Leipzig, and was 
2 CONC buried at St. Paul's church on Dec. 10th.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhardus came to Denmark in 1644 with troops from Bremen to assist King Christina IV of Denmark at the end of the "Thirty Years War" (1618-1648). Though the Danes were subdued he remained in the service of the Danish King and wa
2 CONC s sent to Trondheim Norway in command of the troops there, since Norway was still under the rule of Denmark. Bernhardus married Alide von Bo;ton, daughter of the Mayor of Bremen, and their descendants inter-married with prominent 
2 CONC families in Denmark , Norway and Sweden.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bremen is a port city in northwest Germany, on the banks of the Weser River about 70 km (43 mi) from the North Sea. Bremen has a population of 551,219 (1991 est.). Bremen and its surrounding territory became a Land, or state, of t
2 CONC he Federal Republic of Germany in 1947 and retained that status following German reunification in 1990. The province has an area of 404 sq km (156 sq mi) and a population of 673,700 (1990 est.). 
2 CONT
2 CONT The Weser River is navigable by seagoing craft up to the city's docks. The Bremen docks are second in importance only to those of Hamburg; they line the Weser to the north of the Old Town. A considerable trade in cotton, grain, an
2 CONC d tobacco is conducted in the city. Above the city the Weser River is used by barges and is linked with the canal system of Germany. On the estuary to the north is the outport of BREMERHAVEN, built on land purchased from Hanover. 
2 CONC It was opened in 1830 to accommodate ships that were too large to sail up to Bremen itself. The chief industries of Bremen are shipbuilding, the production of iron and steel, and the manufacture of automobiles, electrical equipmen
2 CONC t, and machinery. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Bremen was founded about AD 787. By the 13th century it was a prosperous trading town, and in 1358 it became a member of the HANSEATIC LEAGUE. It was declared a free city in 1646 and maintained its independence until 1815, when it
2 CONC  merged with the German Confederation. The city was heavily damaged during World War II but has since been completely rebuilt. The Altstadt, or Old Town, retains its Cathedral of Saint Peter, dating in part from the 11th century, 
2 CONC and its Gothic town hall; both were rebuilt after the war. Also still in existence are several other medieval churches and the Guild Hall of the cloth merchants. 
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Sources===
2 CONT
2 CONT * [http://www.nermo.org/slekt/d0048/g0000076.html#I15768 Berent Gerhardsen von Krogh] 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Denne Slægt, som tilforn har boet i Erkebispedømmet Bremen, er i det 17de Aarhundrede kommet til Danmark.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pct. v. Mobbe: „Geschichte u. Landesbeschreibung der Herzogthümer Bremen u. Verden", pag 81-82. - J. P. Manecke: ,Generalsehauplatz des in den Braunschweig Lüneburger Ländern befindlichen Adels`, Thl. I., pag. 244. (Manuscript; He
2 CONC rtugen af Cambridges Bibliothek i Hannover). - Spiel: ,Vaterländisches Archiv", pr. 1821, pag. 295-297.
2 CONT
2 CONT Den første her i Landet var Bernhard v. Krogh.
2 CONT
2 CONT ”Lexicon over adelige Familier i Danmark, Norge og Hertugdømmerne", I. Bd. pag. 304.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bernhard v. Krogh, eller som han i de officielle Akt­stykker kaldes, Bernt v. Kroge eller Berent v. Kroge, fik den 12/4 1663 kongelig Bestalling som Stats-Major „i Trundhjem". Han var gift med Alida v. Bolten. Deres eneste Søn, Ge
2 CONC org Frederik, var Oberst og Regimentschef og eiede Gaarden Talle; han var 3 Gange gift og havde 7 Sønner. - Den ene, Bernhardus, gik i russisk Tjeneste, blev Oberstlieutenant og Generaladjudant hos General Scheremetov; Arnoldus Ch
2 CONC ristian faldt som Lieutenant i fransk Tjeneste; de øvrige indtraadte i den danske Hær. To af dem have efterladt Afkom, nemlig
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Note===
