1 OCCU Oberstløjntnant i Artilleriet, Magasinforvalter Akershus, Toldkasserer i Arendal, Magasinforvalter på Akershus festning
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.snl.no/Lange/slekt_fra_Holstein
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher Andreas Lange, 1766 kopist ved krigsdirektoratet, 1767 fullmektig, fra 1772 magasinforvalter på Akershus festning. Født 14.2 1744 på godset [Deutsch-]Lindau i Kirchspiel Gettorf i Schleswig, † 12.1 1809 på Akershus. Sø
2 CONC nn av godsforvalter Bernt Christopher Lange (ca 1715-ca 1782) og Anna Lucie Ewald (1713-1746). Innvandret: Ansatt som skriverdreng i Kiel 3 år og i grevskapet Rantzau, før han kom med sin arbeidsgiver, generalkrigskommissær i Norg
2 CONC e Petter Voss († 1769), til Akershus 1764. Beskrives av sin sønn, prost Alexander Lange, som 72'' høy smukk, staslig, rødkinnet mild og vennlig mann [maleri se Lange 1917 s. 14], men alltid alvorlig, meget taus, talte kun litt med
2 CONC  barna, var overordentlig flittig og arbeidssom. Selv om han behersket godt norsk, kunne man høre tyskeren, i hans vanligste vending Det var doch ganske forskrækkeligt (Lange s. 15). En god del av faddrene ved hans barns dåp (navn
2 CONC elister hos Lange 1917) hadde tysk familiebakgrunn.
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher Andreas Lange I, head of the Norwegian Lange family, was born at the 
2 CONT
2 CONT Estate Lindau on 14 February 1744 at 1a.m. After his mothers death in child bed with 
2 CONT
2 CONT below mentioned Anna Lange, he was 1 1/4 years old, sent to his mother's sister, who 
2 CONT
2 CONT was married to minister Mathai in Danischenhagen, and was raised by her, even after she 
2 CONT
2 CONT became a widow and lived in Preetz. He stayed with his aunt until she died when he was 
2 CONT
2 CONT 11 years old, and her daughter was married to Regimental Quarter Master Chief Justice 
2 CONT
2 CONT Clauses. He then lived for about 3 years with his father's brother, above mentioned 
2 CONT
2 CONT monastery tax collector Johann Fr. Lange in Schonberg, where his tutor, confirmed him, 
2 CONT
2 CONT in his 14th year, at the time, minister Scheitlie. (1) He then moved to Kiel as "Skriver-
2 CONT
2 CONT Dreng" (apprentice clerk) for "Ober- und Landesgerichtsadvokat" Lindelof. 3 years later 
2 CONT
2 CONT he was hired by the county manager (non-political) in the county Rantzau, Petter Voss, 
2 CONT
2 CONT who in 1764 was named general commissioner of war to Norway (he died on 28 March 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1769), and followed him to Christiania (Oslo), where he from 1 January 1766 was hired 
2 CONT
2 CONT as 2nd clerk at the treasury of the war office. At the same time, his future wife's mother's 
2 CONT
2 CONT brother, Assessor Military Judge Jorgen Brochmann, was hired as cashier. When the war 
2 CONT
2 CONT office was closed down in 1767, he received "vartpenger" (unemployment benefits) and 
2 CONT
2 CONT later advanced to head clerk for general commissioners of war, Voss and Brocker. He 
2 CONT
2 CONT worked there until September 1772, when he was named warehouse manager at Akershus 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fort (Oslo). He was always a generally well-respected and popular man. His son, Rural 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dean Alexander Lange (No. 34) mentions him in this way (2): "My father was a tall and 
2 CONT
2 CONT statue like man (I think he was 72 inches tall), never heavy, but well-proportioned, 
2 CONT
2 CONT without any doubt a very good-looking man with a healthy, colorful complexion which 
2 CONT
2 CONT he kept until his death. I remember he even had a blush on his cheek after his death. He 
2 CONT
2 CONT was a very kind and friendly man, but very serious".
2 CONT
2 CONT Insert Drawing of the house of warehouse manager Lange at Akershus Fort
2 CONT
2 CONT I do not remember ever having heard him laugh, but neither did I see him hot-tempered,
2 CONT
2 CONT scolding, swearing or cursing. The most extreme he ever said, when he thought 
2 CONT
2 CONT something was really bad, was: "that was doch", quite awful. (Doch and och were the 
2 CONT
2 CONT only words he said that made you realize he was a German. By the way, he spoke the 
2 CONT
2 CONT most beautiful Norwegian, and helecturered extremely well and I owe it to him, that I 
2 CONT
2 CONT read and talk quite well) He was very quiet, reticent to us, the children, and as far as I 
2 CONT
2 CONT heard, he was the same to my mother and others. He was extremely diligent and he 
2 CONT
2 CONT industriously worked from morning till evening at his desk in the little closed off room by 
2 CONT
2 CONT the living room. For his no doubt complicated work and accounting, he never had any 
2 CONT
2 CONT other help than an assistant, mostly for running errands. At twilight he walked up and 
2 CONT
2 CONT down the living room floor with his hands in his pockets, idly making noises with his 
2 CONT
2 CONT keys or small changes (coins), However, we children did not stay away from him, on the 
2 CONT
2 CONT contrary, we hung onto his coat while he paced the floors and rode on his foot at 
2 CONT
2 CONT nighttime. I doubt he ever corporally punished us. Only once, when I behaved very badly 
2 CONT
2 CONT and cried because I was not allowed to go with him to Stabaek, did he touch me with the 
2 CONT
2 CONT whip he had in his hand, but that made such a thorough impression on me that I have 
2 CONT
2 CONT never been able to forget it. He loved us all, perhaps me not the least, and I cherish the 
2 CONT
2 CONT highest respect and love for him. He and his family lived a very moderate life - according 
2 CONT
2 CONT to his son Alexander's notes, which state the following: "The way of life was very 
2 CONT
2 CONT moderate. For long periods of time, the children would have thin porridge made of rye 
2 CONT
2 CONT and milk (!) in the morning, later on a serving of sandwiches and tea. Every night the 
2 CONT
2 CONT whole family had rye porridge and milk; only Father and Uncle Brochmann had a 
2 CONT
2 CONT sandwich each."
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) With regards to Schonberg and the Nobel maiden monastery of the time. See notes in the 1st edition, page 9.
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) Minister Alexander Lange's memories about his life and his time (1792-1863)- Published by Christopher Lange, 
2 CONT
2 CONT minister. Char. 1905, page 1
2 CONT
2 CONT If not by all standards a rich man, he worked hard and became a quite well off man. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Which the below included poster shows. As a matter of fact, a burglary was done at his 
2 CONT
2 CONT house, and this poster was probably posted at the Akershus Fort and at various other 
2 CONT
2 CONT places in Chstiania and its nearby vicinity. The poster goes like this:
2 CONT
2 CONT Insert Poster with long list of missing articles  written in Gothic letters
2 CONT
2 CONT About his last years alive and his death, his son the provst, Alexander Lange writes: "The 
2 CONT
2 CONT war in 1808 had certainly increased, but also complicated my father's business". The need 
2 CONT
2 CONT for grain, that turned into a real famine, was the reason why people from far around were 
2 CONT
2 CONT sent to Akershus fort's warehouse to receive small portions of grain (from 1/2 Skj‘ppe 
2 CONT
2 CONT (old measurement, bushel and up) in addition to other things. My father still only had one 
2 CONT
2 CONT assistant to help him and had also assumed the responsibility for a lot of other tasks. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Loads of books were handed over to him and kept in a big closet in one of the bedrooms. 
2 CONT
2 CONT My father finally over strained himself due to the heavy workload and at the end of 1808 
2 CONT
2 CONT he got a serious neural fever. During his illness, I was trusted to sign his name on all 
2 CONT
2 CONT grain orders and, I also think for bank orders, as I had been taught to write his signature 
2 CONT
2 CONT as he did it. However, he recovered, but he started too early to carry out his very 
2 CONT
2 CONT strenuous office work and then Anna Lucie suddenly died at Stab‘k on 8 December and 
2 CONT
2 CONT he was not at all prepared for it. He took it very hard, because he, as the rest of us, loved 
2 CONT
2 CONT this beautiful and loving woman. I clearly remember him, in bed, very emotionally 
2 CONT
2 CONT making arrangements for her funeral saying: "No expenses are too much, she was worth 
2 CONT
2 CONT it all". His illness got worse, his strength was disappearing, and the 3 best doctors in 
2 CONT
2 CONT town, Moeller, Thulstrup and Baumgarten, of whom Thulstrup for a long time came to 
2 CONT
2 CONT see us every night at 12 o'clock, could do nothing. My weak, but always when necessary, 
2 CONT
2 CONT strong and persevering mother was not away from his bed neither day nor night. For 
2 CONT
2 CONT weeks she did not take her clothes off, just had a nap on a bed in the bedroom. My father 
2 CONT
2 CONT had been moved into the living room and the door was open in between. The night of 12 
2 CONT
2 CONT January 1809, she did as usual, watched over him. I don't think I watched over him all the 
2 CONT
2 CONT time, but early in the morning she called for me and asked me to watch over him while 
2 CONT
2 CONT she rested for a while. She strictly ordered me to wake her up in case of any changes. He 
2 CONT
2 CONT lay quietly, almost as if sleeping. It was hard to wake my mother up. But it must have 
2 CONT
2 CONT been even worse for her to be awakened. However, I woke her up when it looked to me 
2 CONT
2 CONT as if my father's breathing was changing. She quietly, full of strength, which I very much 
2 CONT
2 CONT admired, came over to the bed, and affectionately lied down next to him, and said: Don't 
2 CONT
2 CONT you recognize me any longer, father? I don't think he gave any sign and without changing 
2 CONT
2 CONT at all, the noble, faithful husband and father stopped breathing. He did not change at all, 
2 CONT
2 CONT and looked just as good at his deathbed as he did alive. He was buried at the family site 
2 CONT
2 CONT on 20 December at The Old Aker Churchyard. I can't believe how the circumstances in 
2 CONT
2 CONT connection to his funeral have completely vanished from my memories. God praise his 
2 CONT
2 CONT soul and let us meet again in Heaven! My mother carried her great loss with peace and 
2 CONT
2 CONT quiet affection for God and she continued her undiminished and inflexible prayers for we 
2 CONT
2 CONT the children, as already spoken of previously (2) About the conditions in Chnstiania at the 
2 CONT
2 CONT time: The war and the years of scarcity that followed, brought so much need, yes. Real 
2 CONT
2 CONT lack of bread, even among people you wouldn't expect it to occur. Our family, curiously 
2 CONT
2 CONT enough, was not struck by the scarcity. We always had bread, and I don't think we made 
2 CONT
2 CONT any changes in our moderate way of life. However,
2 CONT
2 CONT (1) This place in the cemetery is still owned by the family. Whether his first wife is buried here, is uncertain. However 
2 CONT
2 CONT both he and his second wife and most probably some of his children; are buried there in addition to the manager of the 
2 CONT
2 CONT national archive Lange. Erling Lange. Mrs. M.K. Lange and colonel *Halvard Lange have been buried there since 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1861. *Secretary of State in Norway from 1945-65 (Labor Government).
2 CONT
2 CONT (2) This part, as well as a lot of other stories from provst Lange memories, is not included in the printed edition, they 
2 CONT
2 CONT only exists in his manuscript.
