1 NOTE {geni:about_me} '''Stotts''' History, Family Crest & Coats of Arms
2 CONT
2 CONT The Stotts surname is generally thought to derive from the Middle English word "stott," meaning a "steer," or "bull." As such it was likely originally an occupational name for a cattleman, or perhaps a nickname. Some instances of 
2 CONC the name in Scotland may have come from the Viking name Stoti, which likely arrived with the 9th century Norse colonization of Strathclyde and Aberdeenshire.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early Origins of the Stotts family
2 CONT
2 CONT The surname Stotts was first found in Yorkshire, but by the 15th century some of the family name had moved north to Aberdeenshire where Andrew Stott was a Burgess there in 1490. Later, Margaret Stote held a family seat in Dolphins
2 CONC toun in Lanarkshire in 1675, and Alexander Stote held a family seat in Maynes in Balyeordie in Renfrewshire in 1689.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://www.houseofnames.com/stotts-family-crest
